---
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "@data/site";
const updated = "July 17, 2026";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="OpenBurnBar privacy policy — what data we collect, when, why, who processes it, and how to delete it."
>
  <section class="legalpage">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow eyebrow--ember">Legal</span>
      <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="muted small mono">Last updated: {updated}</p>

      <p class="lead">
        This policy describes how <strong>{SITE.entity}</strong> handles your data when you use OpenBurnBar.
        For an annotated, plain-English version with diagrams, see the <a href="/privacy"
          >Privacy &amp; trust</a
        > page.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Summary</h2>
      <p>
        OpenBurnBar is local-first by default. With no account and no opt-ins, no telemetry,
        analytics, crash reports, or usage data are transmitted from your device. Optional features
        — Firebase cloud sync, Apple-platform iCloud session-log mirroring, BurnBar Cloud, BurnBar
        Cloud Pro, Sentry diagnostics — each require explicit activation.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Information we collect</h2>
      <p><strong>By default:</strong> nothing leaves your device.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>If you sign in and enable Firebase sync:</strong> usage row summaries (tokens, cost estimates,
        timestamps, provider names), provider account metadata and quota snapshots (redacted labels, IDs,
        refresh status, limits, remaining quota), in-app chat thread metadata (thread IDs, titles/previews
        when enabled, timestamps, counts), conversation/session metadata and sync watermarks, shared artifact
        metadata and revisions, and sync state metadata.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>If you have an active BurnBar Cloud or BurnBar Cloud Pro subscription</strong>
        and enable the matching capability: chat message bodies, conversation metadata, session-log manifests
        and chunks, and Cloud Pro cross-device relay traffic (encrypted end-to-end from OpenBurnBar's
        perspective).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>If you enable iCloud session-log mirroring:</strong> copies of selected local session
        log files into the Apple iCloud Drive container
        <code>iCloud.com.openburnbar.app</code>. Mirrored files may contain prompts, assistant
        responses, file paths, and code snippets because they are copies of the originals. This uses
        your Apple ID; it is separate from our Firebase infrastructure.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>If you add a hosted quota account:</strong> provider authentication material that you
        explicitly provide. Secret values are stored in Google Cloud Secret Manager; Firestore stores
        only a redacted label. Hosted quota refresh requires a valid subscription entitlement and may
        be rate-limited.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>If you enable diagnostics:</strong> anonymized crash reports may be sent to Sentry.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Information we never receive by default</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your API keys or credentials for local-only usage tracking.</li>
        <li>
          The content of your source code or agent conversations unless you explicitly enable chat
          backup, session-log backup, or iCloud mirroring.
        </li>
        <li>
          Personal identifying information beyond what your Apple or Google account provides for
          sign-in.
        </li>
        <li>
          Data from unrelated applications. Local usage tracking reads only supported coding-agent
          logs that you make available on the device; those logs are not transmitted unless you
          explicitly enable a matching backup or sync feature.
        </li>
        <li>
          Payment card numbers. Billing is handled entirely by Apple App Store, Google Play, or
          Stripe, depending on where you subscribe — we never receive card details. The Windows
          Store package is free.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>4. How information is used</h2>
      <p>
        Data you opt to sync is used to (a) deliver the feature you enabled (e.g. cross-device chat
        resume), (b) operate the OpenBurnBar service for you, and (c) detect abuse and protect the
        service. We do not sell personal data. We do not use your data to train models. We do not
        engage in cross-app or cross-site tracking.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Sub-processors</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Google · Firebase / Google Cloud:</strong> authentication, Firestore, Cloud Functions,
          Cloud Run, Secret Manager, App Check.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Apple · iCloud Drive:</strong> the iCloud session mirror, when enabled.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Apple · App Store / StoreKit:</strong> subscription billing, JWS receipt issuance.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google · Play Billing:</strong> subscription billing for purchases made on Android.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Microsoft · Microsoft Store:</strong> Windows package signing, acquisition, and updates.
          The Store package is free and does not use Microsoft commerce for subscriptions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stripe:</strong> subscription billing and checkout for purchases made on the web. Stripe
          receives your payment details directly; we receive only the subscription status.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Sentry:</strong> opt-in crash reports.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Amplitude · Analytics:</strong> opt-in product-usage analytics, when enabled in
          <em>Settings → Analytics</em>. Receives feature names, enumerated outcomes, and bucketed
          counts only — never conversation content, prompts, API keys, secrets, or message bodies.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>6. Children</h2>
      <p>
        OpenBurnBar is a developer tool intended for adults. We do not knowingly collect data from
        children under 13.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Your rights and choices</h2>
      <p>You can, at any time:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Delete all local data by removing the app and its support files at <code
            >~/Library/Application Support/OpenBurnBar/</code
          > on macOS or <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\OpenBurnBar\</code> on Windows. Portable Windows builds keep
          their data in the app folder's <code>Data\</code> directory.
        </li>
        <li>
          Delete cloud data by signing out and selecting <em>Delete my data</em> in <em
            >Settings → Account</em
          >.
        </li>
        <li>Disable any optional feature in Settings.</li>
        <li>Remove hosted quota credentials by deleting the provider account.</li>
        <li>Delete iCloud mirrored files in iCloud Drive.</li>
        <li>
          Manage or cancel your subscription where you bought it: <em>Settings → Apple ID</em> on Apple
          devices, Google Play subscriptions on Android, or the Stripe billing portal for web purchases.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>8. Security</h2>
      <p>
        Provider keys live in the macOS Keychain on Apple platforms and DPAPI-protected Windows
        storage on Windows. Hosted credentials, when used, live in Google Cloud Secret Manager.
        Firestore is gated by Firebase Auth, owner-scoped rules, App Check, and a secret-field-name
        denylist. Apple App Store receipts are JWS-verified against pinned Apple root CAs; Microsoft
        Store owns signing and updates for Store-distributed Windows packages. See the <a
          href="/security">security model page</a
        > for the full threat model.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Changes</h2>
      <p>
        We may update this policy as the product evolves. Material changes will be noted in the <a
          href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md`}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">project changelog</a
        > and reflected in the "last updated" date above.
      </p>

      <h2>10. Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Privacy controller: <strong>{SITE.entity}</strong>. <br />
        Email: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.privacyContact}`}>{SITE.privacyContact}</a>. <br />
        Repository: <a href={SITE.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{SITE.github}</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .legalpage {
    padding-block: var(--s-9);
  }
  .legalpage h1 {
    margin-top: var(--s-3);
    margin-bottom: var(--s-2);
  }
  .legalpage h2 {
    margin-top: var(--s-7);
    margin-bottom: var(--s-3);
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    font-weight: var(--wght-semibold);
    --opsz: var(--opsz-lead);
  }
  .legalpage p,
  .legalpage ul {
    color: var(--text-base);
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .legalpage ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-2);
    margin: var(--s-3) 0;
    padding-left: 1.2em;
  }
  .legalpage ul li {
    list-style: disc;
    list-style-position: outside;
    padding-left: 0.4em;
  }
  .legalpage strong {
    color: var(--text-bright);
  }
  .legalpage a {
    color: var(--ember-bright);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .lead {
    margin-top: var(--s-3);
    margin-bottom: var(--s-5);
  }
  .small {
    font-size: var(--t-caption);
  }
</style>
